Document.description_en:
The Pine View Golf Course proposes to demolish its clubhouse building. The building is located within the Pine View Golf Course at 1471 Blair Road in Ottawa. It has reached the end of its lifecycle and should be removed.

&nbsp;

The clubhouse is a one-storey concrete block building, with a suspected mid 1970s construction date. The building houses a dining hall/reception hall and restaurant, locker rooms, various offices, washrooms and mechanical rooms. It has a footprint of approximately 2,000 m3. There is no basement.


The building would be fully demolished. Some excavation would be required to remove the foundation. Vegetation immediately adjacent to the building may be selectively removed where required. The demolition would represent the first phase of a broader initiative, with the proponent intending to rebuild a new clubhouse in the future.

&nbsp;

The purpose of the project is to eliminate immediate and long-term health and safety risks and support the long-term stewardship and future use of the property.
&nbsp;

The proposed project would take place on lands owned by the NCC, and will be subject to a federal land use, design, and transaction approval under the National Capital Act. The NCC will make a determination under the Impact Assessment Act.


The construction area would be accessed with machines and equipment, and all work areas would be fenced accordingly. Construction would take place in the fall of 2026, outside the active nesting period for migratory birds. Construction work would follow all municipal by-laws, including those related to noise.
&nbsp;

Document.description_fr:
Le terrain de golf Pine View envisage de d&eacute;molir son pavillon. Le b&acirc;timent est situ&eacute; au sein du terrain de golf Pine View, au 1471, chemin Blair, &agrave; Ottawa. Il a atteint la fin de son cycle de vie et doit &ecirc;tre d&eacute;moli.

&nbsp;

Le pavillon est un b&acirc;timent d&#39;un &eacute;tage en parpaings de b&eacute;ton, dont la date de construction remonterait probablement au milieu des ann&eacute;es 1970. Il abrite une salle &agrave; manger/salle de r&eacute;ception et un restaurant, des vestiaires, divers bureaux, des toilettes et des locaux techniques. Sa surface au sol est d&#39;environ 2 000 m&sup3;. Il ne comporte pas de sous-sol.

&nbsp;

Le b&acirc;timent serait enti&egrave;rement d&eacute;moli. Des travaux d&#39;excavation seraient n&eacute;cessaires pour retirer les fondations. La v&eacute;g&eacute;tation situ&eacute;e &agrave; proximit&eacute; imm&eacute;diate du b&acirc;timent pourrait &ecirc;tre enlev&eacute;e de mani&egrave;re s&eacute;lective si n&eacute;cessaire. La d&eacute;molition constituerait la premi&egrave;re phase d&#39;une initiative plus large, le promoteur ayant l&#39;intention de reconstruire un nouveau pavillon &agrave; l&#39;avenir.

&nbsp;

L&#39;objectif du projet est d&#39;&eacute;liminer les risques imm&eacute;diats et &agrave; long terme pour la sant&eacute; et la s&eacute;curit&eacute;, et de favoriser la gestion responsable &agrave; long terme ainsi que l&#39;utilisation future du bien immobilier.

&nbsp;

Le projet propos&eacute; serait r&eacute;alis&eacute; sur des terrains appartenant &agrave; la CCN et sera soumis &agrave; une approbation f&eacute;d&eacute;rale de l&#39;utilisation du sol, du design et des transactions immobili&egrave;res en vertu de la Loi sur la capitale nationale. La CCN rendra une d&eacute;cision en vertu de la Loi sur l&#39;&eacute;valuation des impacts.

&nbsp;

L&#39;acc&egrave;s au chantier se ferait &agrave; l&#39;aide de machines et d&#39;&eacute;quipements, et toutes les zones de travail seraient cl&ocirc;tur&eacute;es en cons&eacute;quence. Les travaux de construction auraient lieu &agrave; l&#39;automne 2026, en dehors de la p&eacute;riode de nidification active des oiseaux migrateurs. Les travaux de construction respecteraient l&#39;ensemble des r&egrave;glements municipaux, y compris ceux relatifs au bruit.

